 =?UTF-8?q?1124`:=20the=20across-line=20null=20is=20BOUNDED,=20and=20a=20p?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ositive=20control=20now=20says=20so?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

P3's across-line block reported `{1:0, ..., 7:0}` — no Fano line mapping to a
line under right multiplication by any basis unit. A row of zeros with no
control is indistinguishable from an indexing bug, so this adds `u = 0` (the
real unit e₀) as the POSITIVE CONTROL. It returns 7/7, which makes the seven
zeros a result rather than an artifact.

The verdict is reclassified accordingly, per the null-classification rule
(`[[feedback_an_instrument_that_cannot_return_otherwise_is_not_a_measurement]]`):
**BOUNDED, not REFUTED.** What is measured is that right multiplication is not
the across-line action — and the reason is now stated, because multiplying a
line by one of its own members yields a set containing index 0, which is not a
line at all. What is NOT shown is that no group acts simply transitively on
the 7 lines; that action lives in Aut(𝕆) on the basis structure, which no
shipped op exposes as a frame change.

So the certifiable statement is narrower than "the 28 frames are not a
torsor": `octonion_frame_read` PRESENTS them as a FIBRED SET — 7 bases, each
carrying a 4-element torsor — and only the within-line level is certified
torsorial by anything shipped.

